## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest v4 · **.NET 10** · **Integration scope** (class tagged `[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smells | Affected Tests |
|----------|--------|----------------|
| High | 2 | 2 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **3** | **3** |

5 of 8 test methods are **clean and well-structured** — good AAA separation, meaningful assertions, clear naming, and proper resource cleanup via `[TestCleanup]`.

---

### High Severity

#### 1. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
// ❌ No assertion — passes even if rows are silently discarded
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
```

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk:** Gives false confidence — the test "passes" even if the DB silently drops inserts.

---

#### 2. Conditional Test Logic

**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 101)

```csharp
// ❌ if/else means one branch is never tested
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix:** Write two separate tests with deterministic setup:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "VIP", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com")!;

    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk:** One branch silently goes untested depending on the data path taken at runtime.

---

### Medium Severity

#### 3. Sleepy Test

**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ❌ 3-second wall-clock wait
```

**Integration-test calibration:** Even for integration tests, hard sleeps are fragile — a loaded CI machine may need more time, while locally you waste 3 seconds unconditionally.

**Fix:** Use a polling/retry loop with a timeout:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var notified = await PollUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(notified, "Notification was not sent within timeout");
}
```

**Risk:** Flaky failures on slow CI; wastes wall-clock time on every run.

---

### Positive Observations

| What's Done Well | Examples |
|-----------------|----------|
| Clear naming (`Method_Scenario_Expected`) | All test names follow this pattern |
| Proper resource cleanup | `[TestCleanup]` disposes the connection |
| Hermetic data (in-memory SQLite) | No mystery-guest risk from external DBs |
| Focused assertions per test | `InsertUser`, `UpdateUser`, `DeleteUser` each test one behavior |
| Good concurrent test design | `ConcurrentInserts` properly awaits and asserts |

---

### Prioritized Remediation

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Action |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | ~2 min | Add count assertion to `BulkInsert` |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | ~5 min | Split into two deterministic tests |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | ~10 min | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll-until helper |

**Bottom line:** Your suite is in good shape overall. The 5 clean tests demonstrate solid integration-test design. The 3 findings above are real but straightforward to fix — the highest-priority item (assertion-free test) is a 2-minute change.
